# Car Price Prediction using Machine Learning

## Project Overview

This project focuses on predicting the selling price of used cars based on details such as car name, company, manufacturing year, kilometers driven, and fuel type.
The dataset contains real-world inconsistencies such as missing values, incorrect entries, text mixed with numerical values, and inconsistent formatting. The project covers data cleaning, preprocessing, feature encoding, and building a machine learning model for price prediction.

## Dataset

The original dataset contains **892 car records** with the following columns:

* `name` - Name/model of the car
* `company` - Car manufacturer
* `year` - Manufacturing year
* `Price` - Selling price of the car
* `kms_driven` - Kilometers driven
* `fuel_type` - Fuel type of the car

After cleaning and removing invalid records, the dataset contains **815 records**.

## 🧹 Data Cleaning

The dataset required several preprocessing steps before applying machine learning:

* Cleaned the car name and kept the first three parts of the name
* Converted the `year` column into numeric values
* Removed invalid year entries
* Removed records where the price was listed as `"Ask For Price"`
* Converted car prices from text format to integers
* Cleaned the `kms_driven` column and converted it to numeric values
* Removed invalid kilometer entries
* Removed records with missing fuel type
* Removed extremely high-price outliers above ₹60 lakh
* Saved the cleaned dataset as `Cleaned_car.csv`

## 🤖 Machine Learning

This project uses **Linear Regression** to predict car prices.

The categorical features were converted into numerical features using **One-Hot Encoding**.

### Features Used

* Car name
* Company
* Manufacturing year
* Kilometers driven
* Fuel type

### Target Variable

* `Price`

The data was divided into training and testing sets using `train_test_split`.

A Scikit-learn pipeline was created to combine:

1. One-Hot Encoding
2. Linear Regression

This approach helps handle categorical variables while keeping the preprocessing and model training together in a single pipeline.

## 🛠️ Technologies Used

* Python
* Pandas
* NumPy
* Scikit-learn
* Jupyter Notebook
* Machine Learning
* One-Hot Encoding
* Linear Regression

```

## 🎯 Objective

The main objective of this project is to understand how different car attributes can be used to build a machine learning model for predicting used-car prices.
It also provided practical experience with handling messy real-world data and preparing categorical and numerical features for machine learning.
